1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Good afternoon, everyone!

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:07,779
This is the first seminar in preparation for our archaeological fieldwork in Namibia;

3
00:00:07,779 --> 00:00:14,399
we are fantastically lucky to have received partial research funding for this trip from our Institute,

4
00:00:14,399 --> 00:00:18,950
so I shall expect 200% attention and participation from you all.

5
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:25,605
First in this seminar, I'm going to give a brief introduction to contemporary research on rock art,

6
00:00:25,605 --> 00:00:30,302
and in the second part I'm going to give you some do's

7
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:35,000
and don'ts for our fieldwork trip in April, so please listen very carefully.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,220
I'm first going to focus on the interpretation of rock art in Namibia.

9
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,940
We are very fortunate to be going to an area

10
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:46,690
where you can find some of the most important sites in the entire world.

11
00:00:46,690 --> 00:00:52,139
And I hope to show you how easy it is for everyone to make mistakes in looking at cultures

12
00:00:52,139 --> 00:00:53,700
which are different from our own

13
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:56,440
The first and most important lesson we have to learn.

14
00:00:56,440 --> 00:01:01,000
In Namibia there are both paintings and engravings

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,300
That's where the surface of the rock is cut out.

16
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,317
Many of the engravings show footprints of animals

17
00:01:06,317 --> 00:01:11,973
and most scholars used to think that the purpose of these was simple and obvious:

18
00:01:11,973 --> 00:01:17,629
this rock art was like a school book with pictures to teach children about tracks:

19
00:01:17,629 --> 00:01:21,400
which track belonged to which animal-giraffe, lion and so on.

20
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:23,600
But there were some mysteries.

21
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:27,450
First, when you look at a typical Namibian painting or engraving,

22
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:32,700
you see the tracks are repeated, there are dozens of tracks for the same animal.

23
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:36,720
You'd expect just one clear illustration if the reason

24
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,360
The aim-was to teach tracking.

25
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:42,320
Now there were two more problems.

26
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:51,640
Why are some of the engravings of animals very accurate as you'd expect-all clearly identifiable, and others quite unrealistic?

27
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:56,360
And another mystery some of these unrealistic animals

28
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:00,040
that's in the engravings- seem to be half human.

29
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,730
Some, for example, have got human faces.

30
00:02:02,730 --> 00:02:08,900
Many researchers now think that these were pictures the wise men engraved of themselves.

31
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:12,983
They believed they could use magic to control the animals they had drawn,

32
00:02:12,983 --> 00:02:15,810
so the hunters could then catch them for food.

33
00:02:15,810 --> 00:02:20,907
This shows you some of the dangers of coming from one culture to another,

34
00:02:20,907 --> 00:02:23,820
as we'll be doing, without understanding it fully.

35
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:28,913
Scholars imagined that children looked at rock art pictures to learn to track-just

36
00:02:28,913 --> 00:02:32,047
because they themselves had learnt skills from pictures;

37
00:02:32,047 --> 00:02:37,140
many researchers now believe that rock art had a much more complex purpose.

38
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:39,600
And we'll talk more about it next week!

39
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:44,785
Now before I invite you to join in a discussion in this second part of the seminar,

40
00:02:44,785 --> 00:02:51,190
I'd like to make some very important points about our fieldwork, and in fact any field trip to look at rock art.

41
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:55,620
We're going to a number of sites, and we won't always be together.

42
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:00,990
The single largest problem faced by people who manage the sites is-yes,

43
00:03:00,990 --> 00:03:04,260
I'm sure you've guessed-damage caused by visitors

44
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:06,360
even though it's usually unintentional.

45
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:08,790
Whenever you do go to a site,

46
00:03:08,790 --> 00:03:15,040
don't forget you can learn many things from observing at a distance instead of walking all over it.

47
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,340
This can really help to reduce visitor pressure.

48
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:23,456
People often say, 'Well, there's only two of us and just this one time',

49
00:03:23,456 --> 00:03:27,110
but maybe thousands of people are saying the same thing.

50
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:32,386
And then some basic rules to guide you. We'll have our own camp near a village,

51
00:03:32,386 --> 00:03:37,310
but remember never to camp on a site if you go on your own.

52
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:41,265
It may be disrespectful to the people of that culture,

53
00:03:41,265 --> 00:03:45,220
and certainly don't make fires, however romantic it may seem.

54
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,980
It's really dangerous in dry areas

55
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:52,130
and you can easily burn priceless undiscovered material by doing so.

56
00:03:52,130 --> 00:03:56,560
So, how are we going to enjoy the rock art on our field trip?

57
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,320
By looking at it, drawing it

58
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:03,850
and photographing it-NEVER by touching it or even tracing it.

59
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:07,000
Rock art is fragile and precious.

60
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,920
Remember that climbing on rocks

61
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:14,180
and in caves can destroy in a moment what has lasted for centuries.

62
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:17,220
So no heroics in Namibia, please!

63
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:20,210
Try to be extra careful and help others to be too.

64
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:24,834
And lastly please don't even move rocks or branches to take photographs -

65
00:04:24,834 --> 00:04:31,000
you should leave the site intact -I'm sure I can rely on you to do that.

66
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,743
Well, that's about all I want to say before today's first discussion,

67
00:04:35,743 --> 00:04:39,696
but if you have any questions please ask them now

68
00:04:39,696 --> 00:04:45,230
and don't forget you'll find some fascinating information about world-wide sites on the Internet.

69
00:04:45,230 --> 00:04:47,400
Right, first question then?
